1	At the Cafe	\N	/api/media/at-the-cafe.m4a	Customer: Hi, can I have a coffee, please?\nWaiter: Sure. Anything else?\nCustomer: A glass of water, thank you.	A1	30	1	2026-10-08 13:12:05.496528+00	2026-10-13 13:56:40.907195+00	\N	2	3.5	EDU APP English	original
5	The Bicycle and the City	\N	/api/media/the-bicycle-and-the-city.m4a	Lecturer: Today I want to look at how the bicycle has shaped, and been shaped by, the design of cities.\nLecturer: The first machine that we would recognise as a bicycle appeared in the 1880s. Earlier designs had a huge front wheel, which made them fast but dangerous, and they were ridden mainly by young men. The so-called safety bicycle, with two wheels of equal size and a chain, changed that completely. Because it was cheaper and easier to ride, it was taken up by workers and, importantly, by women, for whom it offered a new kind of independence.\nLecturer: Cyclists quickly became a political force. In several countries, cycling clubs campaigned for better roads, and much of the smooth surfacing that motorists later enjoyed was originally laid because of pressure from cyclists, not drivers.\nLecturer: The twentieth century, of course, belonged to the car. From the 1950s, many cities were redesigned around motor traffic. Wide roads were built through historic centres, and bicycles came to be seen as old-fashioned. In the Netherlands, for example, cycling fell sharply in this period.\nLecturer: What reversed this trend? In the Dutch case, two things. One was a public protest movement in the 1970s against the number of children killed in road accidents. The other was the oil crisis of 1973, which made fuel expensive and led to car-free Sundays. Together these created the political will to invest in separated cycle lanes.\nLecturer: Researchers today talk about four features of a city that encourage cycling. The first is safety, and in practice that means physical separation from cars, not just a painted line. The second is a connected network: a lane that suddenly ends is almost worse than no lane at all. The third is secure parking, because the fear of theft discourages many people. And the fourth is what planners call mixed use, meaning that homes, shops and workplaces are close enough together for cycling to be practical.\nLecturer: The benefits go beyond transport. Studies have found that regular cyclists take fewer days off work through illness, and shops on streets with cycle lanes often see their sales rise, because cyclists tend to stop more often than drivers.\nLecturer: Finally, a word about electric bikes. These have extended cycling to older people and to cities with steep hills, where cycling was previously difficult. However, their higher speed means that the design of lanes may need to change, particularly at junctions.	C1	480	13	2026-10-08 13:12:05.542174+00	2026-10-13 13:56:40.926948+00	4	6	8	EDU APP English (giọng đọc máy)	original
6	Asking for Directions	\N	/api/media/asking-for-directions.m4a	Tourist: Excuse me, is the city museum near here?\nLocal: Yes. Keep going along this street and turn left at the bookshop.\nTourist: Left at the bookshop. Is it far?\nLocal: No. It takes about ten minutes on foot. The museum is next to the post office.\nTourist: What time does it close?\nLocal: It closes at five, but the ticket office shuts at four thirty.\nTourist: Thank you. Is there a bus?\nLocal: Bus number 12 stops outside, but walking is quicker today because of the market.	A2	70	5	2026-10-13 13:22:17.786973+00	2026-10-13 13:56:40.931138+00	\N	3.5	5	EDU APP English (giọng đọc máy)	original
17	The station clock	\N	/api/media/the-station-clock.m4a	Guide: Welcome to Central Station. Before we start the tour, a note about the clock above the main hall. The clock stopped last Tuesday after a storm. Engineers will repair it on Thursday morning, and the hall will stay open while they work.\nPlease use the clocks on the platforms, not the hall clock, until Friday. Trains are still on time. The repair will not close the ticket office. Lost property has moved for this week to the room beside the cafe, on your left as you face the platforms. It closes at six. If you lose a bag today, go there before six, and bring your ticket.	B1	70	41	2026-10-14 02:08:42.295415+00	2026-10-14 02:08:42.295415+00	2	4	5.5	EDU APP English (giọng đọc máy)	original
18	Why cities add salt to roads	\N	/api/media/why-cities-add-salt-to-roads.m4a	Lecturer: When ice forms on a road, tyres lose their grip. Many cities spread salt because salt lowers the temperature at which water freezes. A thin layer of salty water stays liquid a few degrees below zero, so the ice does not bond to the road surface.\nSalt is cheap and easy to store, but it is not harmless. It eats into steel bridges and it washes into rivers, where it can harm freshwater plants. For that reason, some cities now mix salt with sand. The sand gives tyres something to grip, and crews can use less salt.\nThe mixture does not work in every storm. Below about minus ten degrees, ordinary road salt loses most of its effect, and crews switch to a different chemical. Drivers should still slow down. Salt makes a road safer. It does not make it dry.	B2	80	42	2026-10-14 02:08:42.314278+00	2026-10-14 02:08:42.314278+00	4	5	6.5	EDU APP English (giọng đọc máy)	original
20	Choosing a presentation topic	\N	/api/media/choosing-a-presentation-topic.m4a	Mai: We still need a topic for Friday's presentation. I wanted to talk about city bikes, but the tutor said another group has taken transport.\nKhoa: What about the library's new late hours? They stay open until ten on weekdays now.\nMai: That is useful, but it is only a short announcement. We need something we can explain for eight minutes.\nKhoa: Then let's do food waste in the student canteen. I interviewed the manager yesterday, and she said most waste is bread and salad, not meat.\nMai: Perfect. I will make the slides, and you can present the interview. We should bring one chart and finish with three tips.\nKhoa: Agreed. Let's meet at the library at four to practise once.	B1	70	44	2026-10-14 02:28:48.366478+00	2026-10-14 02:28:48.366478+00	3	4.5	6	EDU APP English (giọng đọc máy)	original
21	How bees find flowers	\N	/api/media/how-bees-find-flowers.m4a	Lecturer: A honeybee that finds a rich patch of flowers has to tell the rest of the hive. She does this with a dance on the comb, in the dark, so the other bees follow the movement rather than a picture.\nIf the flowers are close, within about fifty metres, she turns in tight circles. This round dance says the patch is near, but it does not give a direction. For anything farther, she uses a waggle dance. The angle of the straight run, compared with the top of the comb, matches the angle between the sun and the flowers. The length of the waggle tells the distance. A longer waggle means a longer flight.\nThe dance is surprisingly accurate, yet it is not a map. Wind, hills, and a cloudy sky all make the route harder, and bees still search when they arrive in the area. The dance gets them to the right neighbourhood. Their eyes and their sense of smell finish the job.	B2	80	45	2026-10-14 02:28:48.389105+00	2026-10-14 02:28:48.389105+00	4	5	6.5	EDU APP English (giọng đọc máy)	original
31	The Millford flood	\N	/api/media/the-millford-flood.m4a	Lecturer: Today's short talk is about the 2019 flood in Millford. I will name two causes, and two steps the council will take.\nThe flood had two causes. Heavy rain fell for three days, and a new car park covered the field that used to soak up water. The old bridge did not cause the flood. The bridge was high enough. A broken pump at the mill was repaired a week before the rain, so that was not a cause either.\nAfter the flood, some people asked for a higher bridge and for a dam upstream. The council will not build either of those. They will widen the drains under the high street, and they will turn the car park back into a field of grass. They will also keep the Saturday market where it is.	C1	80	55	2026-10-14 03:51:28.146033+00	2026-10-14 03:51:28.146033+00	4	6	7.5	EDU APP English (giọng đọc máy)	original
32	The end of the night buses	\N	/api/media/the-end-of-the-night-buses.m4a	Lecturer: The council has ended the night buses, and the report gives two reasons. After eleven, most buses carried fewer than eight passengers. The depot also cannot staff a third night shift. The fare did not change, so price was not a cause. Roadworks on the ring road finished in May, before the decision. Complaints about noise were few, and the report sets them aside.\nTwo services will take the place of those buses. A shared taxi will leave the station every half hour until one in the morning. On Sundays, the first train will leave at six instead of seven. The old night route will not return. The council will not add a tram, and it will not keep a bus on Friday nights only.	C1	80	56	2026-10-14 04:16:22.475574+00	2026-10-14 04:16:22.475574+00	4	6	7.5	EDU APP English (giọng đọc máy)	original
43	A walk around the research centre	\N	/api/media/a-walk-around-the-research-centre.m4a	Guide: Welcome to the research centre. I will label five places on the plan. You are standing at number 1, the reception, on the south side. Collect your visitor card here. The cafe beside the door is not on the plan.\nOn your left, number 2 is the archive. Files from before 1990 are kept there. The computers in the corridor search only the later records.\nNumber 3, in the middle, is the laboratory. Visitors may look through the glass, but they may not enter.\nOn your right, number 4 is the seminar room. This afternoon's discussion is for staff, not for visitors.\nStraight ahead, at the north end, number 5 is the lecture hall. The public talk starts at four, and a ticket from reception is enough.	C1	70	67	2026-10-14 05:07:26.313438+00	2026-10-14 05:07:26.313438+00	2	6	7.5	EDU APP English (giọng đọc máy)	original
2	Booking a Community Hall	\N	/api/media/booking-a-community-hall.m4a	Clerk: Good morning, Westbrook Community Centre. How can I help you?\nCaller: Hello. I'd like to book the main hall for a birthday party, please.\nClerk: Of course. Can I take your name first?\nCaller: Yes, it's Daniel Fairley. That's F-A-I-R-L-E-Y.\nClerk: Thank you, Mr Fairley. And what date were you thinking of?\nCaller: Saturday the fourteenth of March. Oh, sorry, the fifteenth. The fourteenth is a Friday.\nClerk: That's right, Saturday the fifteenth. How many guests are you expecting?\nCaller: About sixty adults, and maybe twenty children.\nClerk: So eighty people in total. The main hall holds up to one hundred, so that's fine. What time would you like to start?\nCaller: The party starts at seven, but we'd like to get in earlier to decorate. Could we have the hall from five thirty?\nClerk: Yes, five thirty is fine. The hall must be empty by eleven.\nCaller: That's no problem. What does it cost?\nClerk: For a Saturday evening it's forty-five pounds an hour. There's also a deposit of a hundred and fifty pounds, which we return after the event if there's no damage.\nCaller: Is there anywhere to prepare food?\nClerk: There's a fully equipped kitchen next to the hall. You can use the fridge and the oven, but please bring your own plates. We don't provide those any more.\nCaller: OK. And what about parking?\nClerk: There's free parking behind the building for about thirty cars. Your guests can also use the supermarket car park across the road after six o'clock.\nCaller: Great. Do I need to bring anything else?\nClerk: If you're playing music, you'll need to keep the volume down after ten, because of the neighbours. And we'll need a contact phone number.\nCaller: Sure, it's oh seven seven one five, double four two, nine three six.\nClerk: Thank you. I'll email you a booking form today.	B1	360	10	2026-10-08 13:12:05.503271+00	2026-10-13 13:56:40.915653+00	1	4	6	EDU APP English (giọng đọc máy)	original
3	Riverside Park Volunteer Day	\N	/api/media/riverside-park-volunteer-day.m4a	Guide: Good morning, everyone, and thank you for coming to the Riverside Park volunteer day. My name's Helen and I'm the park's volunteer coordinator. Before we split into groups, I'll explain how the day will work.\nGuide: First, a little background. The park opened in 1962 on land that used to be a timber yard, and for many years it was mainly used by dog walkers. Ten years ago the council planned to sell part of it for housing, but local residents campaigned against this, and that campaign is really how our volunteer group started. We now have over two hundred members.\nGuide: Today we'll be working until three o'clock. Lunch is provided, and it will be served at the boathouse, not at the visitor centre as it says on the website, because the visitor centre kitchen is being repaired. If you have any food allergies, please tell me before eleven.\nGuide: Please wear the gloves we give you at all times. You don't need to bring your own tools; everything is in the store room. The one thing we do ask is that you keep your phone switched off near the bird hide, because the noise disturbs the nesting birds.\nGuide: Now, let me tell you about the different areas and what we'll be doing in each one.\nGuide: Starting by the main gate, there's the rose garden. The roses are actually in good condition this year, so the team there will be painting the old benches, which are looking rather tired.\nGuide: Next to it is the children's playground. The equipment was checked last week, so today the job there is collecting litter, which tends to build up around the fence.\nGuide: Moving along the river, you'll come to the wildflower meadow. Many people think we cut the grass there, but in fact we leave it long. Today we'll be planting seeds to add more variety.\nGuide: Beyond the meadow is the pond. A lot of weed has grown over the surface this summer, and that needs to be removed so that light can reach the water.\nGuide: Then there's the woodland walk. Several trees came down in the storms last winter, and the team there will be clearing fallen branches from the path.\nGuide: And finally, the old orchard at the far end of the park. The fruit trees there are very old, and today a specialist will be showing volunteers how to prune them properly.\nGuide: Right, if you'd like to choose your area, come and write your name on the list.	B2	420	11	2026-10-08 13:12:05.511393+00	2026-10-13 13:56:40.919897+00	2	5	6.5	EDU APP English (giọng đọc máy)	original
4	A Research Project on Food Waste	\N	/api/media/a-research-project-on-food-waste.m4a	Tutor: So, Maya and Tom, how's the project on food waste going?\nMaya: Quite well, I think. We decided to focus on student halls rather than restaurants, because we could actually get access to the kitchens.\nTom: And restaurant owners weren't very keen to talk to us anyway.\nTutor: That makes sense. How did you collect your data?\nMaya: At first we wanted to interview students, but we realised people don't really remember what they throw away. So in the end we weighed the food waste bins in four halls every evening for two weeks.\nTutor: Good. And what did you find?\nTom: The biggest category by far was bread. We expected it to be fruit and vegetables, but bread was nearly a third of the total.\nMaya: Mostly because people buy a whole loaf and only eat half before it goes stale.\nTutor: Interesting. Did anything else surprise you?\nTom: The difference between the halls. The hall with shared fridges wasted much more than the ones where each student has their own small fridge.\nMaya: We think it's because in a shared fridge nobody feels responsible, and things get pushed to the back and forgotten.\nTutor: What about the time of the semester?\nMaya: Waste went up sharply in the last week before the holidays, when people were leaving and just emptied their cupboards.\nTutor: Right. Now, in your report you'll need to make recommendations. What have you got so far?\nTom: Well, we've talked about several ideas. One was a sharing shelf in each kitchen, where people can leave food they won't eat.\nMaya: And the other one we really like is selling smaller loaves in the campus shop. Both of those are cheap and easy to set up.\nTom: We also discussed cooking classes, but honestly that would be expensive, and the students who sign up probably aren't the ones wasting food.\nMaya: And we thought about fines for full bins, but we decided that would just make people hide their waste.\nTutor: I agree. So, which sources are you planning to use?\nMaya: For the background, the government report on household waste is really useful, especially the statistics, although it's a bit out of date now.\nTom: The article by Professor Lin is more recent, and it's very good on the psychology of why people waste food, but it doesn't have much data.\nMaya: And there's the campus sustainability survey, which is great because it's specific to our university, but only about a hundred students answered it, so it's not very reliable.\nTutor: Those are fair comments. Make sure they go into your discussion of limitations.	C1	480	12	2026-10-08 13:12:05.524584+00	2026-10-13 13:56:40.923428+00	3	5.5	7.5	EDU APP English (giọng đọc máy)	original
