26	School breakfast clubs	A\nMany primary schools now open their doors half an hour before the first lesson. The room is quiet, the lights are already on, and a small team of staff is setting out bowls. This early session is usually called a breakfast club.\n\nB\nThe clubs began for a simple reason. Teachers saw children arrive hungry, and those children found it hard to concentrate in the first lesson. A meal before school was meant to close that gap, not to replace lunch or to raise scores on a national test.\n\nC\nA typical morning is short. Pupils eat porridge or toast and a piece of fruit. Some read at a side table. Others finish homework they were too tired to do the night before. The club ends when the bell rings, and the same children go to their ordinary classes.\n\nD\nParents pay a small fee for each morning, and the school fund covers the rest. The meal is therefore cheaper than buying food on the way to school, but it is not free for every family. Staff who run the club are paid from the same fund.\n\nE\nSchools that have kept a club for a year report fewer late arrivals, and teachers say the first lesson is calmer. They do not claim that exam results have jumped. The gain they describe is a steadier start to the day.	B2	EDU APP English	12	33	2026-10-14 00:41:36.410539+00	2026-10-14 00:41:36.410539+00	ACADEMIC	Education	5	6.5	original
