33	Four views on city trees	A recent seminar brought together four researchers who disagree about how cities should plant trees.\nDr Lena Ortiz measured air temperature on two narrow streets that were alike in every way except shade. On the street with a continuous line of trees, the afternoon reading was three degrees lower. She argues that this cooling is the clearest benefit cities can expect, and that wide avenues already have enough breeze to need fewer trees.\nProfessor Hasan Malik looked at repair records rather than temperature. Where roots had been planted within two metres of old clay drains, the pipes cracked within a decade. He does not oppose trees, but he insists that the planting line must stay clear of ageing pipes.\nDr Mei Chen followed forty newly planted trees through their first summer. The ones that survived were not those nearest a tap. They were the ones on blocks where a single neighbour had agreed to water them twice a week and to remind the others. Without that person, even well-sited trees dried out.\nProfessor Owen Blake is less interested in how many species a park contains. He tracked small birds and found that they rarely crossed a busy road unless a line of trees or shrubs joined one green space to the next. A long list of species inside one isolated park, he says, does less for those birds than a plain corridor between parks.	C1	EDU APP English	8	46	2026-10-14 03:56:08.227408+00	2026-10-14 03:56:08.227408+00	ACADEMIC	Town planning	6	7.5	original
34	Visitors to the night market	A city university has published a short study of the Saturday night market. Researchers stopped four hundred visitors as they left and asked why they had come.\nTwo reasons dominated the answers. Most people named the food, and almost as many named the live music at the east end of the square. The clothes stalls drew only a small share of visitors. Parking is not free: drivers pay after six, and few people cited it as a reason to come. The last bus currently leaves at nine, which several visitors called too early, but they did not list the bus itself as a reason they had attended.\nThe council has accepted two changes before the next season. It will add litter bins along the food row, and it will hold the last bus until eleven. It will not build a stage, because the musicians already use the existing steps. It will not cut food prices, which the study treats as a matter for the stalls. It will also leave the clothes stalls where they are.	C1	EDU APP English	8	47	2026-10-14 03:58:23.031788+00	2026-10-14 03:58:23.031788+00	ACADEMIC	Town planning	6	7.5	original
