44	The town pool on Saturday	The Saturday swimming club meets at the town pool. The first class starts at nine. Children under ten must come with a parent. Bring a towel and a swim hat. Goggles are optional.\nThe class costs five pounds, and you pay at the desk. After the class, the cafe sells juice until noon. The shop next to the pool is closed on Saturday.	A2	EDU APP English	6	57	2026-10-14 05:11:58.115472+00	2026-10-14 05:11:58.115472+00	GENERAL	Daily life	3.5	5	original
45	Parts of a pollen trap	The diagram shows a pollen trap fitted to a hive for a short study. Point 1 is the roof. It keeps rain off the mesh. Bees do not enter through it.\nPoint 2 is the mesh. Returning bees scrape pollen off their legs as they pass. Honey is not collected here.\nPoint 3 is the drawer. Pollen falls into it and is emptied each evening. The drawer is not a feeder.\nPoint 4 is the stand. It lifts the hive above wet ground. It does not hold the pollen.	C1	EDU APP English	8	58	2026-10-14 05:18:53.40835+00	2026-10-14 05:18:53.40835+00	ACADEMIC	Environment	6	7.5	original
46	Lights in the town park	The town park is lit until midnight, and that habit should change. Paths that stay bright all evening keep people walking home, but the last two hours serve almost nobody. The lights should stay on until ten, and then go off.\nA full switch-off at dusk would be the wrong remedy. In winter the park is already dark by five, and people still cross it after work. They need the paths lit for those hours.\nSome councillors want brighter bulbs. Brightness is not the problem. A stronger lamp at midnight still wastes power and still shines into the houses on Park Road.\nWhether the newer bulbs use less electricity than the old ones is a question for the engineer. It does not change the hour at which the lights should go off.	B2	EDU APP English	8	59	2026-10-14 05:25:56.303628+00	2026-10-14 05:25:56.303628+00	ACADEMIC	Town planning	5.5	6.5	original
47	The school book fair	The school book fair is on Friday in the hall. It opens at ten and closes at three. Parents can come after one. Students visit with their class in the morning.\nBooks cost from two pounds. Stories and comics are on the front table. Dictionaries stay on the shelf by the door. The school does not sell food at the fair. Juice is in the canteen.\nHelpers wear a red badge. New helpers meet Miss Khan at half past nine so she can show them the tables.	A2	EDU APP English	6	60	2026-10-14 05:33:38.339867+00	2026-10-14 05:33:38.339867+00	GENERAL	School	3.5	5	original
48	Late opening at the city library	A\nThe city library used to close at six. Students who work during the day could not get in, and several of them asked the council for later hours.\n\nB\nThe council tried late opening on Thursdays only, until nine. Staff were paid for one extra hour, and two rooms stayed shut to keep the cost down.\n\nC\nIn the first month the reading room was full, but the computers were often free because the wifi failed twice. The trial therefore looked busy and broken at the same time.\n\nD\nA later count showed that most late visitors were students, not shoppers from the high street. The council did not count how many books they borrowed.\n\nE\nNext year the late opening will run on two evenings, but only if the same staff agree to stay. The council will not hire a new night team.	C1	EDU APP English	8	61	2026-10-14 05:41:05.317271+00	2026-10-14 05:41:05.317271+00	ACADEMIC	Education	6	7.5	original
50	The night bus trial	A\nThe night bus trial ran for six weeks in October. Buses left the station every half hour from ten until two. The route followed the river road, not the high street.\n\nB\nShop workers were the people who used it most. They finished at eleven and had been walking home. Students used it less often, because the campus already has a late minibus.\n\nC\nThe council counted passengers by hand at the station door. A ticket machine was fitted, but it was not used for the count. Each bus carried about twelve people on a weekday night.\n\nD\nAfter the trial, the council kept the Friday and Saturday buses only. Weeknight buses stopped because the driver cost more than the fares. The river-road route stayed the same on those two nights.	B2	EDU APP English	8	63	2026-10-14 06:30:21.384728+00	2026-10-14 06:30:21.384728+00	ACADEMIC	Transport	5.5	6.5	original
51	Three sites in the winter bird count	The winter bird count compared three coastal sites. Counters visited each site in a different month and wrote down only the bird that dominated the notes.\nThe harbour wall was counted in January. Ducks were the bird in the notes, and the total was 42. Counters used binoculars. They did not take a hide to the wall.\nThe salt marsh was counted in February. Herons were the bird in the notes, and the total was 18. Counters sat in a hide and left the binoculars behind.\nThe pier was counted in March. Gulls were the bird in the notes, and the total was 7. Counters walked the length of the pier. March was too windy for the hide, and the binoculars stayed in the office.	C1	EDU APP English	8	64	2026-10-14 07:18:22.647537+00	2026-10-14 07:18:22.647537+00	ACADEMIC	Nature	6	7.5	original
52	The market noise study	Researchers measured noise at the Saturday market for four weeks. The music stall was the loudest. People expected the food stall to be louder, but it was not. The book stall was the quietest. The toy stall was only a little quieter than the music stall.\nMeasurements started at nine and stopped at one. They did not continue until three. Volunteers used a meter. Phones were not allowed.\nThe average level was 78 decibels. One Saturday reached 90, but that figure was left out of the average. A short note appeared in March. The full report will be published in June.	B2	EDU APP English	8	65	2026-10-14 07:57:40.240393+00	2026-10-14 07:57:40.240393+00	ACADEMIC	Environment	5.5	6.5	original
53	A trip to the city zoo	The city zoo is open on Sunday. Tickets cost four pounds for adults. Children pay two pounds. The zoo closes at five.\nVisitors can see the lions near the gate. The monkeys are next to the cafe. Please do not feed the animals. Food is sold only in the cafe.\nThe bus to the zoo leaves the station at ten. The ride takes twenty minutes. Bring a hat if the day is hot. A coat is useful only when it rains.	A2	EDU APP English	6	66	2026-10-14 08:04:23.790543+00	2026-10-14 08:04:23.790543+00	GENERAL	Free time	3.5	5	original
54	The village hall floor	The plan shows the village hall. Point 1, at the south edge, is the entrance. Point 2, on the west side, is the kitchen. Point 3, in the middle, is the hall. Point 4, on the east side, is the office. Point 5, at the north end, is the stage. The cloakroom by the door is not on the plan.	A2	EDU APP English	6	67	2026-10-14 08:08:57.792897+00	2026-10-14 08:08:57.792897+00	GENERAL	Town	3.5	5	original
55	The Saturday bus to the beach	The Saturday bus to the beach leaves at nine. It does not leave at eight. It leaves from the station, not from the market. An adult ticket costs three pounds. Children pay one pound. The bus does not stop at the zoo. It stops at the pier.	A2	EDU APP English	6	68	2026-10-14 08:08:57.840124+00	2026-10-14 08:08:57.840124+00	GENERAL	Transport	3.5	5	original
56	A note about the school bell	The school bell should ring at nine. A bell at half past eight is too early, and children would miss the first lesson if they arrived late. The bell should stay loud. A quiet bell is a bad idea, because people at the far gate would not hear it.	A2	EDU APP English	6	69	2026-10-14 08:08:57.863271+00	2026-10-14 08:08:57.863271+00	GENERAL	School	3.5	5	original
57	The new town market	A\nThe market moved from the square to the river car park in May.\n\nB\nIt opens on Friday evening. It does not open on Sunday morning.\n\nC\nFood stalls are at the north end. Clothes are at the south end.\n\nD\nThe council pays for the lights. Stall holders pay for their own tables.	A2	EDU APP English	6	70	2026-10-14 08:08:57.875378+00	2026-10-14 08:08:57.875378+00	GENERAL	Town	3.5	5	original
58	Rules for the town pool	The town pool is open from eight in the morning to six in the evening. Children under five must stay with a parent. They may not swim alone. Goggles are allowed. Food is not allowed in the water. The notice does not give a price.	A2	EDU APP English	6	71	2026-10-14 08:08:57.894455+00	2026-10-14 08:08:57.894455+00	GENERAL	Free time	3.5	5	original
59	Why the old mill closed	The old mill closed in 1994. The river was too low for the wheel, so the stones could not turn. The last product was flour. Bread was baked in the town, not at the mill. The building is now a museum. Visitors enter from the yard. The shop sells postcards. It does not sell flour.	B2	EDU APP English	8	72	2026-10-14 08:08:57.907071+00	2026-10-14 08:08:57.907071+00	ACADEMIC	History	5.5	6.5	original
60	Parts of a rain gauge	The diagram shows a simple rain gauge. Part 1 is the lid. It keeps leaves out. Part 2 is the funnel. Rain runs through it. Part 3 is the tube. It carries the water down. Part 4 is the jar. The jar holds the water so it can be measured. The stand under the jar is not labelled.	B2	EDU APP English	8	73	2026-10-14 08:08:57.919145+00	2026-10-14 08:08:57.919145+00	ACADEMIC	Science	5.5	6.5	original
61	Three nights at the town theatre	The town theatre published its weekend list. On Friday there is a play. It starts at seven, and a ticket costs twelve pounds. On Saturday there is a concert. It starts at eight, and a ticket costs fifteen pounds. On Sunday there is a film. It starts at six, and a ticket costs eight pounds. The Friday event is not a concert.	B2	EDU APP English	8	74	2026-10-14 08:08:57.9303+00	2026-10-14 08:08:57.9303+00	ACADEMIC	Arts	5.5	6.5	original
62	Should the harbour stay open in winter	The winter ferry from the harbour carries hospital staff who finish after the last bus. Closing the harbour in January would remove that crossing. A closure would save fuel, yet the ferry matters more than the fuel saved. The harbour should stay open through January. This note does not discuss the harbour in the next town.	C1	EDU APP English	8	75	2026-10-14 08:08:57.940929+00	2026-10-14 08:08:57.940929+00	ACADEMIC	Transport	6	7.5	original
63	Who runs the Saturday club	The Saturday club needs four helpers.\nAnna collects the coats at the door.\nBen makes the tea in the kitchen.\nCara meets new members and shows them the hall.\nDan locks the hall at the end. Eve wanted to play the piano, but a pianist is already booked. Omar is away.	B1	EDU APP English	7	76	2026-10-14 08:10:53.299748+00	2026-10-14 08:10:53.299748+00	GENERAL	Community	4	5.5	original
64	Changes at the town library	A\nThe library now opens at nine on weekdays. It used to open at ten.\n\nB\nChildren may borrow ten books. Adults may borrow six.\n\nC\nThe computers are free for one hour. After that you must book again.\n\nD\nA lost card is replaced at the desk. There is no charge on the first visit. A second card costs two pounds.	B1	EDU APP English	7	77	2026-10-14 08:10:53.339877+00	2026-10-14 08:10:53.339877+00	GENERAL	Education	4	5.5	original
